---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About the astro-seo-enforcer demo and its checks"
  description="Why this demo exists, what each route proves about the integration, and how a single failed rule turns the local build and the CI pipeline red."
>
  <h1>About this demo</h1>
  <p>
    This is a second route, so the duplicate-title, duplicate-description and
    duplicate-heading rules have something real to compare against. Its title,
    meta description and <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> differ from every other page in
    the site, which is exactly what those cross-page checks want to see on a
    templated set of pages.
  </p>
  <h2>How the check runs</h2>
  <p>
    After Astro writes the static files, the integration walks the output
    directory, indexes every page and asset, and parses each HTML document once
    with a lightweight parser. Per-page rules run immediately. A handful of
    checks are deferred until every file has been seen: duplicate titles,
    descriptions and headings, near-duplicate bodies, orphan pages that nothing
    links to, and coverage against the generated XML sitemap.
  </p>
  <h3>Failure behaviour</h3>
  <p>
    On a violation the build prints a grouped, colourised report to standard
    error and exits with a non-zero status code. In this demo the threshold is
    set to <code>warning</code>, so any finding at all is enough to fail the
    build. That is stricter than the default, where only errors stop a build and
    warnings are printed for you to triage at your own pace.
  </p>
  <h3>What this route adds</h3>
  <p>
    Beyond giving the duplicate checks a partner, this page contributes an inbound
    link to the home page and to the nested article, so the orphan-page check can
    confirm that the internal link graph actually connects. It also carries the
    same JSON-LD block as every other route, which keeps the structured-data rule
    satisfied that the markup is present and parses cleanly. Return to the
    <a href="/">demo home page</a> or continue to the
    <a href="/blog/first-post/">first article</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>Keeping the example honest</h3>
  <p>
    Each time the rule set grows, this demo grows with it: a new check that would
    fire on these pages is a signal that the pages, not the check, need attention.
    That is the whole point of wiring the integration into the demo build at the
    strictest threshold rather than trusting the unit tests alone.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
